  "slug": "untitled-topic-29c0",
  "summary": "New preprints model transcript spillover, contamination, label uncertainty and prediction confidence in spatial and single-cell transcriptomics.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is explicit modeling of technical error in spatial and single-cell transcriptomics. In imaging-based platforms, Dancer uses a reference-guided probabilistic framework to separate within-cell-type heterogeneity from transcript contamination. SYNDRI, a transformer trained on simulated data, removes transcript spillover. SLUICE (R/Seurat) uses screening label uncertainty to separate robust from fragile cell-type composition changes in Xenium data after TBI. For histology-to-expression inference, REGAIN attaches per-prediction confidence and reports gains in cell-type annotation and prognostic value in TCGA-BRCA. A noise-corrected correlation metric (RSC) is proposed for benchmarking such models. Adjacent work addresses data fidelity more broadly: benchmarking of sample-mispairing detection, scRefine's contrastive atlas integration, RecurSieve's detection of non-mean expression differences, and Tumor2Line's optimal-transport tumor–cell-line alignment. CHO transcriptome compendia, UCE state bridges and lung adenocarcinoma Global Tumor Time are peripheral. All are preprints, so reported gains are unreviewed.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
